GET * HTTP/1.1       -> status=404 body="{\"message\":\"Not Found\"}\n"
```
and with `httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodOptions, "*", nil)` (which yields `URL.Path == "*"`, `URL.RawPath == ""`):
```
OPTIONS *  -> code=404 body="{\"message\":\"Not Found\"}\n"
GET *      -> code=404 body="{\"message\":\"Not Found\"}\n"
```
`Router.Find` direct probes (routes: `/*`, `/:id`, `/`, or none):
```
no routes      Find("GET","*")    -> path="*"  params=[] handler=NotFoundHandler err=code=404
/* route       Find("GET","*")    -> path="*"  params=[] handler=NotFoundHandler err=code=404
/:id route     Find("GET","*")    -> path="*"  params=[] handler=NotFoundHandler err=code=404
OPTIONS /*     Find("OPTIONS","*")-> path="*"  params=[] handler=NotFoundHandler err=code=404
/* route       Find("POST","/")   -> path="/*" params=[*] handler=MethodNotAllowedHandler err=code=405
```
So a `"*"` request-target never matches anything (not even `/*`), and `Find` leaves `ctx.path == "*"` with the default `NotFoundHandler`; 405 is never produced for `"*"` because no node is reached. Note the stdlib parses `OPTIONS *` as `RequestURI="*"`, `URL.Path="*"`, `URL.Opaque=""`, so `GetPath(r)` (echo.go:916) returns `"*"` — but a real net/http server answers `OPTIONS *` itself before Echo sees it.

